{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it shall be purified; only with the water of niddah shall it be cleansed.</b> Rabba bar Avuh said: even new utensils, as is in the Jerusalem Talmud at the end of Avodah Zarah. A case came before Rabbi Yirmeya; he said: immersion is required. Why? Because they went out from the impurity of the Canaanites and entered the sanctity of Israel. Rav Nakhman said: Rabba bar Avuh said — they only taught this regarding purchased goods that were seized into the hand of Israel, like the matter that happened with Midian, where the utensils were seized for Israel; but borrowed ones — no. <i>Manei de-khunyah</i> — <i>plumae</i> in the vernacular: their beginning is of clay, and they coat them with a coating of bone, and their use is by means of cold liquid. When we take them from the Canaanites, we follow their end: they are coated with metal, and it is a metal utensil, and it requires immersion. What about a pledge? Mar bar Ashi said: a Canaanite pledged a silver cup to Abba, and he immersed it and drank from it. And I do not know whether it is because he holds that a pledge is like a sale, or because the Canaanite intended to sink it, never to redeem it again. Thus it is taught, and thus Rashi explained at the end of tractate Avodah Zarah."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase מאני דחוניה פלומי״א is rendered with both its transliterated Aramaic form and the vernacular gloss plumae as they appear in the source text; the exact referent of the term is uncertain but the passage describes clay vessels coated with bone and then metal."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:20:26.841678813Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}